I’ll inspect the specimen metadata and pattern first, then identify candidate phases from the frozen reference index and build/refine a complete multiphase GSAS-II model. I’ll preserve physical checkpoints and only pursue residual families that remain coherently unassigned.

The chemistry is PbO + WO₃ heated at 500 °C, so Pb–W–O products plus residual precursors are the chemically ordinary starting inventory. The scan is a dense Cu Kα₁/Kα₂ reflection pattern from 10–100° 2θ; I’m now extracting peak families and locating exact-composition Pb–W–O references before any analogue search.

Three coherent families are already evident:

- scheelite-type PbWO₄ accounts for the dominant series (27.70, 33.05, 45.02, 51.48, 55.54, 72.27°);
- monoclinic WO₃ accounts for the 23.38/23.88/24.64° triplet and its higher-angle family;
- orthorhombic massicot PbO accounts for 29.34/30.62/38.12/53.39/60.53/63.28°.

These assignments have broad-family support, not just isolated peak matches. I’m moving them into a joint refinement and will test whether any coherent residual family remains afterward.

The joint refinement is stable and all three phase scales remain positive. At this checkpoint the modeled crystalline fractions are approximately 79% PbWO₄, 12% WO₃, and 10% PbO, with Rwp down to 15.3%. The remaining mismatch is mostly profile/intensity structure rather than new unmatched peak positions; I’m now separating instrumental broadening from phase size/strain and checking residual families before touching atomic coordinates.

The first exact-composition branch, monoclinic PbWO₄ (raspite), refined to essentially zero abundance and did not explain the residual family, so I’m rejecting it. I’m screening the remaining Pb–O and W–O references against the full residual profile now; any candidate must improve several separated reflections and retain a positive scale in GSAS-II.

The residual screen supports a second PbO polymorph: tetragonal litharge refines to a stable ~2.7 wt% contribution and improves separated peaks across 29–78°. The dominant unresolved peaks at 25.92, 30.14, and 31.04° are not explained by litharge. I’m testing oxidized Pb precursor phases before any analogue-derived tungstate; a candidate will be rejected if it needs implausible cell distortion even when its numerical fit improves.

An exact structural analogue resolves the residual family: COD 7220560, Pb₂MoO₅ (C2/m), becomes chemically plausible Pb₂WO₅ by the single isovalent Mo⁶⁺→W⁶⁺ substitution. Its calculated reflections match the unresolved series across the entire scan—25.70, 29.11, 30.45, 30.89, 34.84, 39.25, 43.22, 47.24, 51.87, 54.74, 60.02, 64.36, 71.62, and 78.05° before the common specimen shift. I’m adding this physically screened analogue now; this is the justified cross-chemistry step because the exact Pb₂WO₅ composition is absent from the frozen index.

Both analogue-derived tungstates pass the acceptance tests. Pb₂WO₅ lowers Rwp from 14.89% to 12.28% with only ~0.5% cell changes from Pb₂MoO₅ and physical tetrahedral W–O distances. An ordered Pb₃WO₆ model derived from Ba₃WO₆ then lowers Rwp to 11.55% at ~3.1 wt%, with all W–O/Pb–O contacts physical and a stable positive scale. I’m checking the six-phase residual once more; if no new coherent family remains, I’ll stop phase search and refine this accepted inventory.

The accepted inventory remains stable through renewed cell/calibration refinement: fractions are currently about 63.5% PbWO₄, 14.1% Pb₂WO₅, 9.9% WO₃, 6.6% massicot PbO, 3.1% Pb₃WO₆, and 2.7% litharge PbO. Zero and specimen displacement both remain within the laboratory’s physical ranges. The phase-specific size/strain refinement is still running; that is the key step for replacing the earlier nonphysical negative instrumental U term.

No additional phase family remains after the six-phase model; the largest residuals coincide with accepted reflections and have peak-shape/intensity signatures. Calibration-only tests give only a marginal change, so I’m now screening the five physically allowed March–Dollase axes for each phase in parallel. This will distinguish real texture corrections from coordinate/profile mismatch before atomic refinement.

The texture model is supported, lowering Rwp to 10.65%; heavy-coordinate refinement reaches 9.81%. Before accepting that gain, I’m auditing the refined bond geometry and coordinate displacements against the physical parent structures. The calibration terms moved but remain within the stated ranges; because zero and displacement are strongly correlated, I’ll retain them only if the structural audit and a rollback comparison support the checkpoint.

The six-phase inventory is now fully indexed and the best physical checkpoint is at Rwp ≈ 9.81%. I’m finishing the coordinate rollback tests, checking that every exported CIF has the intended stoichiometry and geometry, then I’ll package and cross-verify the GSAS-II project, profiles, fractions, and report.

All oxygen-coordinate branches failed the physical rollback criterion: each worsened the fit, and litharge’s oxygen coordinate was numerically unconstrained. I’m retaining the heavy-atom-only checkpoint and treating those oxygen results as rejected tests, not as phase evidence.

One integrity check caught a real issue before packaging: the COD litharge entry was imported with the wrong origin-choice transform, giving a nonphysical Pb₂O multiplicity in GSAS-II. I’m repairing that phase to the correct P4/nmm PbO special positions and will re-refine from the accepted checkpoint; this can legitimately change both its intensity contribution and the normalized fractions.

The repaired PbO model both restores the correct stoichiometry and improves the fit substantially (Rwp 9.07%, versus 9.81% before the repair). Its refined contribution remains positive and stable. I’m now auditing the newly refined bond geometry and testing only a few specimen-level terms that the measurement model supports; the corrected checkpoint is already a complete fallback.

The geometry audit found that the first monoclinic WO₃ reference itself contains an impossible 1.58 Å O–O contact inherited from an old approximate COD model. Because the prompt prioritizes physical structure over a small fit gain, I’m replacing it with the exact-composition, same-volume P2₁/c neutron structure (COD 2311041), then rechecking fit and geometry rather than submitting the defective reference.

The replacement WO₃ structure fits nearly as well, but its unconstrained W-coordinate branch shortened one bond to 1.60 Å, so that branch is rejected. The source neutron coordinates are physical; I’m keeping them fixed and screening preferred orientation in the new crystallographic setting, where the old texture axis cannot be transferred directly.

The optional specimen-term screens are negative: zero, displacement, and polarization change Rwp by less than 0.00005, while surface roughness collapses to its lower bounds with a singular 100% correlation. I’m rejecting those additions and keeping the simpler physical checkpoint. No coherent residual reflection family remains; the remaining mismatch is profile/intensity-level.

The accepted model is now converged over all 8,282 supplied points (Rwp 9.073%, Rp 6.595%, GOF 2.598; max shift/esd 0.017). I’m at the packaging stage: exporting CIFs directly from that exact checkpoint, propagating scale covariance to statistical fraction errors, and validating that every file replays to the same profile and statistics.

The deliverables are now generated from one checkpoint, and the independent GSAS-II forward replay exactly reproduces all six phase fractions and Rwp (9.0732%) without profile review flags. I’m doing the final schema/structure cross-check now, including CIF occupancy, stoichiometry, and full-grid CSV validation.

Analysis complete. Crystalline fractions, normalized to 100% crystalline material:

| Phase | wt% |
|---|---:|
| PbWO₄, scheelite | 55.69 ± 0.22 |
| Pb₂WO₅, monoclinic model | 15.25 ± 0.20 |
| WO₃, monoclinic | 9.34 ± 0.16 |
| PbO, litharge | 8.33 ± 0.12 |
| PbO, massicot | 7.34 ± 0.14 |
| Pb₃WO₆, ordered model | 4.05 ± 0.08 |

Final fit: Rwp 9.073%, Rp 6.595%, GOF 2.598. Independent GSAS-II replay reproduced the fit and fractions without profile-geometry warnings.

